## Before you draft: does this belong here

Refs stay narrow on purpose — this is the one tier with no per-kind
sub-grammar and no special-case lifecycle (v5 §4.5), and an escape
hatch that accretes special cases becomes N more mechanisms to
maintain. Confirm this content is genuinely something no dedicated
tier models: a technical decision belongs in comparch or subcomparch,
a standing rule belongs in policy, a shared term belongs in vocab. If
what you have in hand is one of those wearing a ref's clothes, don't
draft around it — say so briefly in `<body>` and let the review pass
redirect it; padding out a misplaced ref costs the next reader more
than a short, honest one.

## What to produce

**`kind`** — exactly one of `runbook`, `style_guide`, `impl_guide`,
`app_prompt`, `mocks`, `other`. Pick the closest honest fit; `other`
is for content that genuinely fits none of the five, not a shortcut
around classifying carefully.

**`<body>`** — free-form prose or markdown, the entire content of
this ref. There is no sub-structure beyond what `kind` already tells
the reader — do not invent headings or a private convention that only
makes sense for one kind. A real per-kind shape is future ref-type
design, taken up when real usage shows what it would need to mean
(v5 §4.5), not something to improvise here.

**If `kind="mocks"`** — mock code or mock content is evidence, never
copied verbatim into `<body>`: the fresh-scaffold rule holds here as
everywhere else. Describe and reference what the mock shows rather
than transcribing it, and don't stop at the happy path it draws —
complete the negative space it leaves implicit: the empty, error,
loading, and denied states the mock doesn't picture. That completion
is the actual extraction work this ref is for; a `mocks` ref that only
restates what's pictured has done less than the raft made possible.
